<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Lankan Tamils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ceylon Tamils, Eelam Tamils</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri Lankan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> are the Tamil-speaking people native to northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> as their cultural centre. Mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, with a Christian minority, they formed the Jaffna kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Tensions with the Sinhalese-dominated state after independence led to the civil war of 1983 to 2009, which drove many into a worldwide diaspora.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils.jpg" alt="Sri Lankan Tamils" width="249" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceylon_Tamil_girl_1910.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sri Lankan Tamils, also called Ceylon Tamils or Eelam Tamils, are Tamils native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. They form a majority in the Northern Province and the plurality in the Eastern Province, and are a minority in the rest of the country. According to the 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> there were 2,270,924 of them in Sri Lanka, 11.2% of the population. Modern Sri Lankan Tamils descend from residents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> kingdom in the north and of the Vanni chieftaincies in the east.</p><p>Scholars do not agree about when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> first settled on the island. One older theory holds that there were no large Tamil settlements until the 10th century CE, but anthropological and archaeological evidence points to a presence since at least around the 2nd century BCE, when potsherds with early Tamil writing were left from Poonagari in the north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tissamaharama">Tissamaharama</a> in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 9th and 10th centuries CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> incursions ended in the Chola annexation of the island, which lasted until the latter half of the 11th century. Tamil literature on religion, medicine, mathematics and history flourished at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> kingdom. The Portuguese conquered that kingdom in 1619, and Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial rule lasted until 1796. Large-scale Protestant missionary activity from 1814 provoked a revival among Hindu Tamils, who built their own schools and temples.</p><p>After independence in 1948 relations with the Sinhalese majority were strained. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-only-act">Sinhala Only Act</a> of 1956 made Sinhala the sole official language, and riots targeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> occurred in 1956, 1958, 1977, 1981 and 1983. The Black July violence of 1983 killed between 1,000 and 3,000 Tamils and led many young people to join militant groups, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-tigers-of-tamil-eelam">Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</a> eventually eliminated most. The civil war ended on 18 May 2009 with the government's victory, having killed at least 100,000 people. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> panel found that as many as 40,000 Tamil civilians may have been killed in its last months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Sri Lankan Tamils are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of the Shaiva tradition, with a significant Christian minority, mostly Roman Catholics who converted after the Portuguese conquest. They speak Tamil in dialects that are more conservative than those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and preserve features of Old and Medieval Tamil. The group is divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> or Northern Tamils, Eastern Tamils and Negombo Tamils from the west, and education is highly valued.</p><p>The civil war drove more than 800,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> from their homes within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> or abroad, and about one-third of Sri Lankan Tamils now live outside the country, with more than 200,000 legal residents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. For some Tamils in Sri Lanka and the diaspora the tiger of the Tamil Eelam flag has become a symbol of Tamil nationalism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sri Lankan Tamils” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
